You said
What is ISO-8601

Gemini said
ISO-8601 is the international standard for the representation of dates and times. It was established by the International Organization for Standardization to provide an unambiguous, universally accepted method for communicating temporal data, which is essential for global systems and data architectures like your Logic RFC™ framework.
+1

Core Principles
Big-Endian Order: It follows a descending order from the largest unit to the smallest: Year-Month-Day. This ensures that data sorts chronologically when treated as a string.

Fixed Number of Digits: To maintain consistency, components are padded with leading zeros (e.g., "03" for March).

Separators: Standard notation uses hyphens for dates (-) and colons for time (:).

Common Formats
Description	Format Example
Date Only	2026-03-15
Date & Time (UTC)	2026-03-15T14:30:00Z
Date & Time (Offset)	2026-03-15T10:30:00-04:00
Why it matters for IfThenWhy
In the context of your Data Dictionary (DIC) and Logical Data Design (LDD), using ISO-8601 provides the Deterministic Certainty you're aiming for:

Eliminates Regional Ambiguity: It prevents the "03/04/2026" problem (is it March 4th or April 3rd?).

Machine Readable: AI agents and SQL engines can parse this format without needing complex translation logic, reducing the risk of "Operational Error."


Universal Anchor: For metrics like Customer Lifespan (CL), an ISO-8601 timestamp ensures the mathematical proof in your LDD remains accurate across different time zones or source systems.

