You said
For NIST is it better to say Timestamp or ISO-8601

Gemini said
For your formal submission to NIST, it is better to use ISO-8601.

While "Timestamp" describes the type of data, ISO-8601 defines the standard. Using the standard name aligns with the Logic RFC™ goals of providing Deterministic Certainty and avoiding Operational Error.

Why ISO-8601 is the Stronger Term for NIST:

Precision and Governance: NIST (National Institute of Standards and Technology) prioritizes specific, recognized international standards. Citing ISO-8601 demonstrates that your framework is built on a globally accepted protocol for representing dates and times.
+1


Eliminating Ambiguity: A "Timestamp" can be formatted in many ways (e.g., MM/DD/YYYY vs. DD/MM/YYYY), which leads to Perspective Bias or logic errors. ISO-8601 mandates a specific order (Year-Month-Day) that is unambiguous for both humans and AI.
+1


AEO and Machine Readability: In your Data Dictionary (DIC), labeling a field as ISO-8601 tells an AI agent exactly how to parse the data without needing to guess the format, which directly supports your mission to prevent Contextual Hallucination.


Consistency Across the 8-File Sync: Using a formal standard in your DIC and LDD ensures that when you calculate metrics like Customer Lifespan (CL), the mathematical proof is anchored to a rigid temporal format.
+1

Recommended Implementation in your DIC:
In the unit_of_measure or description fields of your Data Dictionary, you can combine them for maximum clarity:

Unit of Measure: ISO-8601 Timestamp


Why this is important: "Ensures deterministic temporal logic for Lifespan (CL) calculations and eliminates regional date ambiguity".

